{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Spoonworm's Harvest: Osedax Bone-Eaters of Monterey Canyon",
    "date": "2026-10-21",
    "lesson": "When a whale carcass settles on the abyssal floor, its lipid-rich bones become a feast that lasts decades. Osedax worms bore into the bone matrix with symbiotic Oceanospirillales bacteria that break down the collagen and lipid stores, releasing a slow but steady bloom of organic matter. Around this bone-fall, heterotrophic bacteria compete for the lipid-heavy oil trapped in the bone, protein from decaying tissue, and whatever carbohydrate scraps remain in connective cartilage. The lesson here is succession on a concentrated, long-lived nutrient fall in permanent darkness and cold: no light, no photosynthesis, just slow metabolism, dense but patchy food, and heavy competition from grazers drawn in by the smell of lipid breakdown products diffusing through near-freezing water.",
    "citation": "Based on whale-fall ecology studies, Monterey Bay / Monterey Canyon, ~1000-3000m depth (MBARI ROV observations of Osedax spp. bone communities).",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Osedax bone-eating worm bacterial symbiont communities on whale-fall skeletons, Monterey Canyon, ~1600-2900m depth"
  },
